# /dep-update — dependency hygiene

**Shared rules in `{{harness.doctrine_path}}` — read it if not already in context** (§4 gates, §6
branch and commit policy).

{{> overlay?:dep-update-landmines}}

1. **Survey:** `{{deps.outdated}}`{{#if deps.audit}} and `{{deps.audit}}`{{/if}}. Patch/minor bumps of leaf packages are routine; a
   major, a framework, or anything with a native build step is not — take those **one at a time**,
   so a failure can be attributed.
2. **Branch** (§6), then update: `{{deps.update}}` for the routine set; name packages for the rest.
   Read the install output, not just the exit code — a silently skipped patch or a native rebuild
   against the wrong ABI exits 0 and breaks at runtime.
3. **Advisories:** {{#if deps.audit_fix}}`{{deps.audit_fix}}` if needed, **never with a force flag** — forcing installs semver-major
   changes nobody reviewed.{{/if}}{{#unless deps.audit_fix}}fix each on its own terms.{{/unless}} An advisory only a major bump clears is a separate change —
   surface the tradeoff.
4. **Gates (§4).** A red gate is one of two opposite things: a **stale expectation** (the dependency
   legitimately changed its output — update the test and say so in the commit) or a **real
   regression** (pin back and file it; don't bless it). Can't tell which → surface it.
5. **Commit once** (§6) with explicit paths: {{deps.manifests_md}}, plus any test a stale
   expectation required. A formatter bump that reformats untouched files gets its own commit.
6. **Report** what moved, what's held back and why, and gate status. **Don't push** — a dependency
   bump lands on {{repo.human}}'s schedule.

{{#if deps.lockfile}}**Lockfile drift with nothing outdated** (`{{deps.install}}` rewrote `{{deps.lockfile}}` with no version change) is a legitimate
standalone commit — say so; don't bury it in another diff.{{/if}}{{#unless deps.lockfile}}This repo has no configured
lockfile: work from {{deps.manifests_md}} and don't create one as a side effect.{{/unless}} Nothing outdated → say so and stop.
